---
import "../styles/global.css";
---

<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
  <head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8" />
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1" />
    <meta
      name="description"
      content="Progwise is building the next-generation platform for developers and their customers to create, maintain, and evolve software with AI agents, engineering guardrails, and human expertise."
    />
    <meta name="theme-color" content="#ffffff" />
    <meta
      property="og:title"
      content="Progwise — You shape. Agents build. Experts guide."
    />
    <meta
      property="og:description"
      content="Explore the Progwise platform in development for developers and their customers to create, maintain, and evolve software with AI."
    />
    <meta property="og:type" content="website" />
    <meta property="og:url" content="https://www.progwise.net/" />
    <link rel="canonical" href="https://www.progwise.net/" />
    <link rel="icon" href="/favicon.svg" />
    <title>Progwise — You shape. Agents build. Experts guide.</title>
  </head>
  <body>
    <a class="skip" href="#main">
      Skip to content
    </a>
    <header class="wrap">
      <a class="brand" href="#" aria-label="Progwise home">
        <img
          class="brand-signet"
          src="/planet-progwise.svg"
          width="28"
          height="29"
          alt=""
        />
        progwise
      </a>
      <nav aria-label="Main navigation">
        <a href="#platform">The platform</a>
        <a href="#how">How it works</a>
        <a href="#contact">Let’s talk</a>
      </nav>
    </header>
    <main id="main">
      <section class="hero">
        <p class="eyebrow">The next chapter of building software</p>
        <h1>You shape. Agents build. Experts guide.</h1>
        <p class="intro">
          A shared platform for developers and their customers to create,
          maintain, and evolve software with AI.
        </p>
        <p class="status">
          Progwise is in development. This is the experience we’re building.
        </p>
        <p>
          Our ambition is a first version in days, followed by years of
          continuous improvement, with lower delivery and maintenance costs.
        </p>
      </section>
      <section class="section" id="platform">
        <h2>Your projects. Your customers. One shared process.</h2>
        <h3>For developers and software companies</h3>
        <p>
          Set up customer workspaces, Git repositories, AI agents, and
          deployment environments. Establish permissions, reviews, tests, and
          engineering standards. Invite customers and designers, oversee
          delivery, and bring your expertise where it’s needed.
        </p>
        <h3>For business people and designers</h3>
        <p>
          Describe what you need. Progwise asks questions, suggests ideas, and
          prepares decisions you can approve, decline, or refine. You shape the
          business outcome. Your development team handles technical choices and
          explains their impact on cost, timing, and the experience.
        </p>
      </section>
      <section class="section" id="how">
        <h2>From the first request to the next release.</h2>
        <p>
          Start by setting up a project and inviting your customer. The same
          process takes you from the first version through every improvement.
          Project decisions guide the work, and available prepaid funding
          determines when chargeable work can start.
        </p>
        <figure class="lifecycle">
          <figcaption>How an active project evolves</figcaption>
          <ol>
            <li>
              <strong>Start the project.</strong> Set the engineering rules and
              invite the customer.
            </li>
            <li id="next-request">
              <strong>Request.</strong> Describe a need, report a problem, or
              explore an idea. The AI consultant asks questions and proposes an
              outcome.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Issue.</strong> Agree on the work and what counts as done.
              Split it when parts can be delivered separately.
              <span class="lifecycle-note">
                Waiting for a decision or funding? Resume when it’s available.
              </span>
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Change.</strong> Agents build on a short-lived branch, run
              checks, and prepare a preview. Get the required reviews before
              merging.
              <span class="lifecycle-note">
                ↺ Needs revision? Update the same change and review again.
              </span>
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Release.</strong> Deploy the approved work and record what
              went live.
              <a class="lifecycle-note" href="#next-request">
                ↺ Next improvement → Request
              </a>
            </li>
          </ol>
          <p class="lifecycle-archive">
            <strong>Archive the project.</strong> End active work when it’s time
            to retire the project. Keep its decisions, changes, and release
            history.
          </p>
        </figure>
        <p>
          Each new request starts with the project’s existing decisions, code,
          tests, and history. Development teams can bring in specialists when a
          change needs their judgment.
        </p>
        <details>
          <summary>How we plan to protect existing software and data</summary>
          <p>
            Limited agent permissions, isolated previews, automated tests, and
            specialist review provide safeguards. Backups, tested data
            migrations, and verified recovery procedures protect existing data.
            Release plans cover monitoring and recovery for both software and
            databases.
          </p>
        </details>
        <h3>Meet in the project office</h3>
        <p>
          Every meeting takes place in a shared virtual office with rooms and
          people you can walk up to. Meet your developer, AI consultant, and
          specialists, review previews together, and turn conversations into
          recorded decisions and work.
        </p>
        <h3>Keep control of spending</h3>
        <p>
          Project work is funded monthly in advance and starts after payment.
          Progwise tracks commitments and spending, suggests specialists,
          updates forecasts, and warns when funds run low. Further chargeable
          work pauses when funding runs out, and additional spending needs your
          agreement.
        </p>
        <p class="status">
          Project funding is separate from the platform subscription. Platform
          pricing is still being developed.
        </p>
      </section>
      <section class="section" id="contact">
        <h2>Help shape what comes next.</h2>
        <p>
          Build software for customers, or shape the tools your business uses?
          We’d like to hear how you work.
        </p>
        <a href="mailto:info@progwise.net?subject=The%20next%20chapter%20of%20building%20software">
          info@progwise.net
        </a>
      </section>
    </main>
    <footer class="wrap">
      <a class="brand" href="#">
        <img
          class="brand-signet"
          src="/planet-progwise.svg"
          width="28"
          height="29"
          alt=""
        />
        progwise
      </a>
      <span>© {new Date().getFullYear()} Progwise Ltd.</span>
    </footer>
  </body>
</html>
